  "brief_md": "## A15: LLM tool in CI to pick blocking test suites and flag flaky tests\n\n**Idea as written:** An LLM tool that sits in a CI pipeline, proposes which test suites should block a given change, and flags likely-flaky tests. Target customer: engineering teams with slow or flaky CI. Source: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46345827\n\n### Payer\nLikely payer is an Engineering/Platform Engineering lead with budget authority over CI tooling. Price hypothesis: $20-50/developer/month, anchored to Harness's AI Dev Assistant add-on pricing (https://vendorbenchmark.com/vendors/harness-platform-pricing). The closest direct competitor, Develocity, prices per-committer-per-year but has no public number — it's sold via contact-sales/free-trial (https://develocity.ai/pricing/), implying this category skews toward larger negotiated enterprise deals rather than self-serve SMB pricing.\n\n### Competitors\n- **Develocity (Gradle Technologies)** — Predictive Test Selection, per-committer/year, contact sales (https://develocity.ai/product/predictive-test-selection/, https://develocity.ai/pricing/)\n- **Harness Test Intelligence** — bundled in Harness CI; AI add-ons $20-50/dev/month (https://www.harness.io/products/continuous-integration/test-intelligence)\n- **BrowserStack Smart Test Selection** — included in Automate/App Automate plans (https://www.browserstack.com/docs/automate/playwright/smart-test-selection)\n- **Testhide** — free self-hosted Docker, Cloud from $49/mo, includes a dedicated \"Flakiness Predictor\" ML model (https://testhide.com/)\n- **ci-sherlock-agent** — open source, free (https://github.com/slavkurochkin/ci-sherlock-agent)\n- Additional indie/OSS entrants found during research in the same period: flaketriage, ai-flake-sleuth, flaky-detector-agent — none monetized, suggesting a crowded, low-differentiation space (https://github.com/AleksandrJelohhin/flaketriage, https://dev.to/debashish_ghosal/your-flaky-tests-arent-flaky-1301, https://github.com/golikovichev/flaky-detector-agent)\n\n### Acquisition channel\n1. GitHub Marketplace / CI platform marketplace listing — free to list, self-serve discovery (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/github-marketplace/selling-your-app-on-github-marketplace/pricing-plans-for-github-marketplace-apps)\n2. Developer content marketing / organic HN & Reddit discussion — cost is mostly founder time; comparable self-serve dev-tool SaaS CAC benchmark is $200-$700/customer (https://ltvcacbook.com/blog/cac-benchmarks-2026)\n\n### Demand evidence\n- \"We knew we had flaky tests because we were experiencing failures, but the tool revealed the full extent of the issue.\" — Federico, TLM at Trunk, describing adopting an internal flaky-test dashboard (https://hackernoon.com/we-analyzed-202-million-ci-jobs-in-trunk-flaky-tests-heres-what-we-learned-part-1)\n- \"Ability to sort by its impact (blocking PRs) and automatically creating tickets for fix saves a lot of time.\" — r/programming thread on managing flaky tests (https://www.reddit.com/r/programming/comments/1hqsipv/how_to_manage_flaky_tests/)\n- Trunk's analysis of 20.2M CI jobs found flaky tests are a top driver of blocked PRs and lost engineering hours even at Google, Meta, Spotify, and Uber (same HackerNoon source)\n\n### Case for\nA solo builder with AI agents can plausibly ship a working MVP fast, because the core technique — ingest JUnit/test history, correlate failures with the scope of a code change, flag statistically flaky tests, and recommend a minimal blocking subset — has already been proven feasible by several one-person open-source projects launched in the same window (ci-sherlock-agent, flaketriage, ai-flake-sleuth, flaky-detector-agent). Distribution has a near-zero-cost channel via GitHub Marketplace, and the pain is well-documented and widely felt, including at companies too small to justify Develocity's or Harness's enterprise contracts — leaving room for a cheap, fast, self-serve wedge priced below the incumbents.\n\n### Case against\nThe space is already crowded with funded incumbents (Develocity, Harness, BrowserStack) and a wave of near-identical indie/OSS clones appearing in the same months, suggesting thin differentiation — the underlying technique is standard statistical pattern-matching on CI history and doesn't strictly require an LLM, so free alternatives can match the core function (https://semaphore.io/can-ai-detect-flaky-tests-or-predict-build-failures-in-ci-cd). More critically, the buyer's core requirement is trust: industry sources explicitly warn that an AI test-selector must never silently let a real failure through, because false negatives destroy the exact trust the tool is meant to restore (https://qaskills.sh/blog/ai-flaky-test-detection-guide, Semaphore above). Earning that trust typically requires months of track record plus deep CI telemetry integration that incumbents already have, and new installs face a cold-start problem with no historical data to train on — a hard thing to solve in a six-week build.\n\n### Open questions\n- Would teams trust an LLM to actually gate/skip tests, or only use it as an advisory signal?\n- How much of this need is already met for free by existing OSS/indie tools, and would teams pay for a hosted version?\n- Which CI platforms need native zero-config integration first, and how much work is each integration?\n- What false-negative rate is tolerable before a team lets the tool gate merges, and how much historical data is needed to reach it?\n- What is real willingness to pay from small/mid teams versus enterprises already locked into Develocity/Harness contracts?\n\n### Regulatory setup\nIf the tool sends source code, test names, or stack traces to a third-party LLM API, enterprise customers will likely require a Data Processing Agreement and security review before granting pipeline access to their codebase — an ongoing obligation; a single legal consult to draft a standard DPA/subprocessor disclosure should cover most SMB deals, revisited if selling into regulated verticals.\n\n### Unsourced claims\n- A solo founder's realistic time-to-MVP for this feature set is six weeks [unsourced]\n- Founder time cost for content-marketing acquisition is effectively free/opportunity-cost only [unsourced]\n"
